Title: THE CALL OF THE WILD
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from the book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

EXT. SNOWY RIDGE - NIGHT

A campfire burns fiercely against the Arctic dark. An old, grizzled prospector, the NARRATOR, sits by the flames, staring into the dancing light.

NARRATOR
Old longings nomadic leap, chafing at custom's chain; again from its brumal sleep wakens the ferine strain.

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - DAY

A sprawling, sun-kissed Santa Clara Valley estate. 

BUCK, a magnificent one-hundred-and-forty-pound St. Bernard and Scotch shepherd mix with a thick furry coat, patrols the wide-spreading lawns. He carries himself in right royal fashion.

MANUEL, a gardener's helper, approaches Buck. He holds a stout rope.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing. Because men, groping in the Arctic darkness, had found a yellow metal.

Manuel doubles the rope around Buck's neck. Buck accepts it with quiet dignity. 

NARRATOR (V.O.)
These men wanted dogs, and the dogs they wanted were heavy dogs, with strong muscles by which to toil, and furry coats to protect them from the frost.

Manuel hands the end of the rope to a shadowed STRANGER. Money chinks between them. The stranger twists the rope. Buck gasps, springing forward in quick rage, but the stranger grapples his throat and throws him backward.

INT. BAGGAGE CAR - NIGHT

Buck is locked inside a cagelike crate. His eyes are blood-shot. He hurls himself savagely against the wooden bars, snarling and frothing.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For two days and nights he neither ate nor drank. He was metamorphosed into a raging fiend.

EXT. SEATTLE BACKYARD - DAY

A high-walled yard. The MAN IN RED SWEATER, stout and grim, drives a hatchet into Buck's crate, prying off the wood. He drops the hatchet and shifts a heavy club to his right hand.

Buck, hair bristling, mouth foaming, launches his one hundred and forty pounds of fury straight at the man.

The club swings. A shocking impact. Buck crashes to the ground. 

He leaps again. Again the club smashes him down. Blood flows from his nose and mouth. He crawls to his feet, dazed, and rushes a final time. The man strikes a shrewd, calculated blow. Buck crumples, utterly senseless.

Buck's eyes flutter open. He lies in the dirt. The Man in Red Sweater fearlessly pats Buck's battered head.

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction, and the best thing we can do is to let it go at that. You've learned your place, and I know mine. Be a good dog and all'll go well and the goose hang high. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you. Understand?

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was beaten, but he was not broken. He saw, once for all, that he stood no chance against a man with a club. It was his introduction to the reign of primitive law.

EXT. DYEA BEACH - DAY

White, mushy flakes fall through the air. Buck sniffs the snow curiously, licking it from his nose. 

FRANCOIS, a swarthy French-Canadian half-breed, fastens an arrangement of straps and buckles onto Buck. PERRAULT, a weazened French-Canadian, watches closely.

SPITZ, a big, snow-white husky, sits in the lead position of the sled team, his scarlet tongue lolling out in a sort of laugh.

FRANCOIS
Mush!

Buck digs his feet in, pulling the heavy sled. Spitz growls a sharp reproof, throwing his weight to jerk Buck into line. 

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here was neither peace, nor rest, nor a moment's safety. They were savages, all of them, who knew no law but the law of club and fang.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

The sled cuts across glaciers and snowdrifts. Buck toils in the traces, his muscles hard as iron. He bites the ice out from between his toes. 

NARRATOR (V.O.)
His development was rapid. He achieved an internal as well as external economy. Instincts long dead became alive again. In vague ways he remembered back to the youth of the breed.

EXT. FROZEN LAKE - NIGHT

A bleak camp on the ice. Buck rests in a snug nest dug into the snow. 

Spitz approaches, teeth bared, and steps into Buck's nest. 

The beast in Buck roars. He springs upon Spitz with a fury that surprises them both. They circle back and forth, fang clashing fang. 

Spitz slashes Buck's shoulder, leaping lightly away. Buck is streaming with blood, panting hard. The silent, wolfish circle of sled dogs waits to finish off whichever dog goes down.

Buck rushes, sweeping low to the snow. His teeth close on Spitz's left fore leg. A loud crunch of breaking bone. Spitz faces him on three legs. 

Buck repeats the trick, breaking the right fore leg. Spitz staggers, snarling with horrible menace. Buck springs in, shoulder squarely meeting shoulder. Spitz goes down beneath the dark circle of the pack.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Mercy was a thing reserved for gentler climes. Buck stood and looked on, the successful champion, the dominant primordial beast who had made his kill and found it good.

EXT. FROZEN LAKE - MORNING

Perrault packs the camp. Francois brings a veteran dog forward to the lead position. Buck springs upon the dog, driving him back, and stands in the leader's place.

FRANCOIS
Eh? Look at dat Buck. Heem keel dat Spitz, heem t'ink to take de job. Go 'way, Chook!

Francois drags Buck aside. Buck retreats, snarling, keeping just out of reach. Francois grabs a heavy club. Buck dodges, refusing to take his old place in the rear.

PERRAULT
T'row down de club.

Francois drops the club. Buck trots in, laughing triumphantly, and swings around into the lead position. 

EXT. SKAGUAY - DAY

Muddy spring streets. Buck and the team stand in the traces, perambulating skeletons. Their coats are limp and matted. 

HAL, a youngster with a big Colt's revolver, and CHARLES, a middle-aged man with watery eyes, lash a mountainous, top-heavy load of unwashed dishes and a massive tent onto the sled. 

MERCEDES, pretty and soft, flutters in their way.

HAL
Mush! Mush on there!

The dogs strain against the breast-bands, but the sled holds like an anchor. They stand still, panting. Hal lashes them savagely with his whip.

MERCEDES
Oh, Hal, you mustn't!

Mercedes drops to her knees, putting her arms around Buck's neck, tears in her eyes.

MERCEDES
You poor, poor dears, why don't you pull hard? Then you wouldn't be whipped.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

The blazing sun beats down on the rotting ice of the river. JOHN THORNTON sits on a log, whittling an axe-handle. 

The exhausted team staggers into camp and drops down as though struck dead. Hal glares at Thornton.

HAL
They told us up above that the bottom was dropping out of the trail. They told us we couldn't make White River, and here we are.

THORNTON
The bottom's likely to drop out at any moment. Only fools, with the blind luck of fools, could have made it. I tell you straight, I wouldn't risk my carcass on that ice for all the gold in Alaska.

HAL
That's because you're not a fool, I suppose. All the same, we'll go on to Dawson. Get up there, Buck! Hi! Get up there! Mush on!

The whip flashes out. The other dogs make painful efforts to rise. Buck makes no effort. He lies quietly. Hal exchanges the whip for a heavy club, raining blows upon Buck's ribs. 

Thornton drops his whittling. He springs upon Hal, hurling him backward. 

THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. Get out of my way, or I'll fix you. I'm going to Dawson.

Thornton raps Hal's knuckles with the axe-handle, knocking his knife to the ground. Thornton takes the knife and cuts Buck's traces.

Hal, Charles, and Mercedes force the remaining dogs onto the ice. Mercedes rides atop the heavy sled. 

Thornton kneels beside Buck, searching for broken bones. 

Out on the river, the back end of the sled drops. Mercedes screams. A whole section of ice gives way. Dogs and humans disappear into a yawning hole. 

Thornton and Buck look at each other.

THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks Thornton's hand.

EXT. THORNTON'S CAMP - DAY

Summer. Buck's muscles have swelled out, his furry coat shining. He lies at Thornton's feet, looking up into his face with pure adoration. 

Thornton takes Buck's head roughly between his hands, shaking him back and forth. Buck's mouth laughs, his throat vibrant.

THORNTON
God! You can all but speak!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time. But in spite of this great love he bore John Thornton, the strain of the primitive remained alive and active.

EXT. STREET IN DAWSON - DAY

Sixty below zero. Hundreds of furred and mittened men bank around a sled loaded with twenty fifty-pound sacks of flour. The runners are frozen fast to the hard-packed snow.

Thornton stands near the sled, his face pale. Buck is harnessed to the heavy load. 

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Thornton had been hurried into the wager, heavy with doubt. A thousand dollars that Buck could start a thousand pounds. Half a ton.

Thornton kneels down by Buck's side. He takes Buck's head in his two hands, resting cheek on cheek.

THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck whines with suppressed eagerness. He seizes Thornton's mittened hand between his jaws, pressing in with his teeth, then releasing slowly. 

Thornton steps well back.

THORNTON
Now, Buck. Gee!

Buck swings to the right, plunging forward. The load quivers. A crisp crackling arises from the runners.

THORNTON
Haw!

Buck duplicates the manoeuvre to the left. The crackling turns into a snapping. The sled pivots.

THORNTON
Now, MUSH!

Buck throws himself forward. His great chest is low to the ground, his claws scarring the hard-packed snow in parallel grooves. The sled sways, trembles, and lurches ahead. Half an inch. An inch. Two inches. 

It moves steadily along. The crowd bursts into a deafening roar. Hats and mittens fly into the air. 

Thornton falls on his knees beside Buck, tears streaming frankly down his cheeks, shaking him back and forth.

EXT. DEEP WOODS - DAY

A broad valley. Thornton washes a pan of dirt, the gold showing like yellow butter. Moose-hide bags of gold are piled like firewood outside a spruce-bough lodge.

Buck sits by the fire, his ears twitching. 

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The months came and went. And closely akin to the visions of the hairy man was the call still sounding in the depths of the forest. It filled him with a great unrest and strange desires.

Buck rises, slipping cat-footed into the trees. 

He comes to an open place. Erect on its haunches, nose pointed to the sky, sits a long, lean timber wolf. 

Buck stalks into the open. The wolf flees. Buck follows in a frenzy, cornering the wolf in a blind channel. Buck circles him with friendly advances. The wolf sniffs noses with him. They run side by side through the sombre twilight.

EXT. THORNTON'S CAMP - DAY

Buck breaks through the brush, dropping down into the valley toward camp. 

He stops. The silence of the forest is pregnant. 

He creeps to the edge of the clearing. The spruce-bough lodge is wrecked. YEEHATS, indigenous hunters with spears and arrows, are dancing about the wreckage. 

Buck sees Thornton's dog, Nig, lying dead with an arrow through his body. 

A gust of overpowering rage sweeps over Buck. He roars aloud with a terrible ferocity, a live hurricane of fury, hurling himself upon the Yeehats. 

He springs at the chief, ripping his throat wide open. He bounds to the next, tearing, rending, destroying in constant and terrific motion. The Yeehats panic, shooting one another with their own arrows, fleeing in terror to the woods.

Buck prowls the desolated camp. He follows a trace to the edge of a deep pool. The water is muddy and discolored. He stares into the water. 

NARRATOR (V.O.)
John Thornton was dead. It left a great void in him, somewhat akin to hunger. But he had killed man, the noblest game of all, and he had killed in the face of the law of club and fang.

EXT. SNOWY RIDGE - NIGHT

The Narrator stares into the dying embers of his fire.

NARRATOR
The last tie was broken. Man and the claims of man no longer bound him. The Yeehats tell of a Ghost Dog that runs at the head of the pack. They are afraid of this Ghost Dog, for it has cunning greater than they.

EXT. FOREST - NIGHT

A full moon rises high over the trees, bathing the snow in ghostly day. 

A pack of timber wolves pours into a clearing in a silvery flood. In the centre stands Buck, massive, motionless as a statue. 

The old, gaunt leader of the pack comes forward. He sniffs noses with Buck, sits down, and breaks out the long wolf howl. 

Buck sits down. He points his nose at the moon and howls. 

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the long winter nights come on, he may be seen running at the head of the pack through the pale moonlight, leaping gigantic above his fellows...

Buck runs side by side with his wild brother, leading the pack through the trees, his great throat a-bellow.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
...his great throat a-bellow as he sings a song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.
